  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nWinds have turned to the northwest behind a dry cold front. \nSunny skies abound with only faint high clouds passing over on \nGOES visible satellite imagery. Current high temperatures in the\n70s will fall once again overnight to lows in the upper 40s for\nrural areas and lower 50s in the Chicago urban heat island.\n\nAn upper level ridge over the western CONUS will slowly slide\neast over the next several days. Seasonable temperatures with \nhighs in the upper 60s to upper 70s are expected tomorrow and \nFriday. Conditions should remain dry for most of the forecast \narea. The exception being a plume of elevated moisture and \nbetter lapse rates that moves in Friday morning along the \neastward periphery of the ridge as it enters the forecast area. \nShowers and perhaps an isolated rumble of thunder are possible \nfor areas mainly northwest of the Fox River Valley, but given \nthe amount of antecedent dry air at the surface the confidence \nin coverage remains low and the chances for precipitation \ndiminish farther east into the forecast area. Winds are \nexpected to turn to the south behind the moisture plume into \nSaturday.\n\nThis will affectively usher in a drier, warmer air mass once \nagain allowing temperatures over the weekend to climb back above\nnormal with possible highs in the low 80s for areas along and \nsouth of I-88 on Saturday. Temperatures will be warm on Sunday \nas well, but could be more muted due to the increased cloud \ncover from the remnants of the tropical system, currently \nchurning in the Gulf.\n\nTropical Storm Isaias is currently northwest of the Yucatan\npeninsula with a forecasted track by the National Hurricane\nCenter to move inland near the Alabama Gulf Shores and head\nnorth toward the Ohio River Valley. The earliest remnants of\nthis system are expected near the forecast area on Sunday\nmorning. Models are still showing some disagreement on the exact\ntrack, but they area starting to favor a northwestern jog that\nwould bring tropical moisture and the chance for showers back\ninto the area. For now, the best chances for rain looks to be\nsoutheast of Interstate 55 and into northwest Indiana, but \nmodel trends will need to be monitored for exact timing and \nlocation.\n\nAfter brief upper level ridging early in the week next week\nfeaturing a bump in temperatures above normal, another cold\nfront is expected to pass through mid week with another chance\nof rain and dropping temperatures back down to seasonable norms.\n\nDK",
